  <h1>THREAD — Single-Brand Fashion Commerce REST API</h1>
  <h2>Admin Domain Comprehensive Technical Manual & Defense Guide</h2>
  <p><strong>System Role:</strong> Developer D — Administrator & Security Infrastructure Owner<br>
     <strong>Course:</strong> CSC4161 Advanced Programming in Web Technology<br>
     <strong>Project Repository:</strong> THREAD Commerce API (NestJS + TypeScript + PostgreSQL + TypeORM)<br>
     <strong>Date:</strong> November 2026</p>

  <div class="callout-success">
    <div class="callout-success-title">Executive Summary of Owned Modules</div>
    <p>As <strong>Developer D (Admin)</strong>, you own the entire security, identity, role governance, and administrative oversight infrastructure for THREAD. Your modules are <strong><code>auth</code></strong>, <strong><code>users</code></strong> (staff administration), <strong><code>roles</code></strong>, <strong><code>audit</code></strong>, and <strong><code>analytics</code></strong>. You also authored shared core security plumbing: password hashing with bcrypt, JWT token signing/verification, RBAC guards with custom decorators, atomic database audit transactions, and platform-level aggregation analytics.</p>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>1. Domain Boundaries & Architectural Responsibilities</h2>
  <p>In accordance with <code>PROJECT_SOURCE_OF_TRUTH.md</code> (§3 & §5) and <code>AGENTS.md</code>, each team member owns a specific business slice of the system. Here is how your Admin domain fits into the 4-tier retail architecture:</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style="width: 20%;">Domain / Role</th>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Owned Feature Modules</th>
        <th style="width: 30%;">Core Business Responsibilities</th>
        <th style="width: 25%;">Strict System Boundaries</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Admin (You)</strong><br><span class="badge badge-admin">Role D</span></td>
        <td><code>auth</code>, <code>users</code>, <code>roles</code>, <code>audit</code>, <code>analytics</code></td>
        <td>Shared authentication, JWT issuing, RBAC enforcement, staff account provisioning, soft-deletions, state-diff audit logging, executive KPI analytics.</td>
        <td>Cannot mutate inventory balances directly, cannot alter product prices, and cannot bypass financial transaction state machines.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Inventory Manager</strong><br><span class="badge">Role C</span></td>
        <td><code>inventory</code>, <code>stock-movements</code>, <code>fulfillment</code>, <code>shipments</code></td>
        <td>SKU balance tracking (<code>on_hand</code>, <code>reserved</code>), stock receipts, manual adjustments, picking/packing orders, courier shipment dispatch.</td>
        <td>Cannot edit catalog prices, create staff, or author refunds.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Customer</strong><br><span class="badge">Role A</span></td>
        <td><code>accounts</code>, <code>addresses</code>, <code>cart</code>, <code>checkout</code>, <code>orders</code>, <code>payments</code>, <code>size-recommendations</code></td>
        <td>Customer profile, saved addresses, cart mutation, transactional multi-table checkout, unpaid order expiry worker, rule-based sizing advice.</td>
        <td>Cannot access staff routes, alter stock counts, or change order status directly.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Staff (Merchandising)</strong><br><span class="badge">Role B</span></td>
        <td><code>catalog</code>, <code>categories</code>, <code>collections</code>, <code>size-guides</code>, <code>returns</code></td>
        <td>Product management, variants/SKUs, category taxonomy, seasonal collections, size chart ranges, customer return review.</td>
        <td>Cannot create staff accounts, modify inventory receipts, or alter DB roles.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr>

  <h2>2. Security & Authentication Architecture (<code>auth</code>)</h2>

  <h3>2.1 Bcrypt Password Encryption</h3>
  <p>THREAD enforces strict zero-knowledge credential storage. Passwords are never stored in plain text, logged, or returned in API responses.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salt Rounds:</strong> <code>12</code> rounds via <code>bcrypt.hash(password, 12)</code> (exceeds standard 10 rounds for high entropy against rainbow tables and brute-force attacks).</li>
    <li><strong>Verification:</strong> <code>bcrypt.compare(candidatePassword, user.passwordHash)</code> runs during authentication in <code>AuthService.login()</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Sanitization:</strong> Database selects explicitly exclude <code>password_hash</code> on public or administrative profile outputs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2.2 JSON Web Token (JWT) Lifecycle</h3>
  <p>The authentication system issues signed, tamper-evident Bearer JWTs upon successful credential verification:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Signing Algorithm:</strong> HMAC-SHA256 (<code>HS256</code>) with a strong, 32+ character secret verified at application boot via <code>env.validation.ts</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Payload Shape:</strong> Minimal claims to prevent token bloat: <code>{ "sub": "&lt;user_uuid&gt;" }</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Token Lifespan:</strong> 1 day (<code>1d</code>) expiry.</li>
    <li><strong>Verification Mechanism:</strong> <code>JwtStrategy</code> (Passport JWT) intercepts requests with the <code>Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;</code> header, decodes the <code>sub</code> claim, verifies the UUID format, and ensures the user exists and has <code>status = ACTIVE</code> in PostgreSQL. If the user was deactivated, the token is rejected immediately with <code>401 Unauthorized</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2.3 Privilege Escalation Prevention</h3>
  <div class="callout-warning">
    <div class="callout-warning-title">Critical Security Defense Point</div>
    <p>Public registration at <code>POST /api/v1/auth/register</code> accepts email, full name, and password. It strictly forces <code>role: UserRole.CUSTOMER</code>. Even if a malicious user injects <code>"role": "ADMIN"</code> into the registration payload, the DTO strips or ignores it, and the service hardcodes the customer role. Staff and Admin accounts can <strong>only</strong> be created by an authenticated administrator through <code>POST /api/v1/admin/staff</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>3. Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) System (<code>roles</code>)</h2>

  <h3>3.1 Role Hierarchy & Policies</h3>
  <p>The application defines four distinct authenticated roles plus an unauthenticated Guest browsing mode:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>ADMIN</code>: Total oversight over personnel, audit logs, and platform analytics.</li>
    <li><code>INVENTORY_MANAGER</code>: Stock intake, warehouse balance management, pack/ship logistics.</li>
    <li><code>STAFF</code>: Merchandising, catalog authoring, categories, and customer returns.</li>
    <li><code>CUSTOMER</code>: Normal shopper actions (cart, checkout, address book, own order tracking).</li>
    <li><em>Guest:</em> Unauthenticated caller (catalog search, public product views, login/registration).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3.2 Custom Decorators & Guards</h3>
  <p>The Admin domain implemented custom declarative security primitives:</p>

  <h4>A. <code>@Roles(...)</code> Decorator (<code>src/modules/roles/roles.decorator.ts</code>)</h4>
  <p>Attaches metadata to controller classes or route handlers defining which roles are permitted to access the endpoint:</p>
  <pre>export const Roles = Reflector.createDecorator&lt;UserRole[]&gt;();</pre>

  <h4>B. <code>RolesGuard</code> (<code>src/modules/roles/roles.guard.ts</code>)</h4>
  <p>Reads the route metadata using NestJS <code>Reflector</code>. Extracts the authenticated user identity populated by <code>JwtAuthGuard</code> and evaluates whether <code>roles.includes(user.role)</code>. If false, execution terminates immediately with a client-safe <code>403 Forbidden</code>.</p>

  <h4>C. <code>@CurrentUser()</code> and <code>@Actor()</code> Decorators</h4>
  <p>Extracts the calling actor's identity, IP address, and User-Agent from the HTTP execution context so services can record attribution without coupling business logic to Express <code>Request</code> objects:</p>
  <pre>// Usage in StaffController:
@Post()
async createStaff(
  @CurrentUser() user: AuthenticatedUser,
  @Body() input: CreateStaffDto
) {
  return this.users.createStaff(user.id, input);
}</pre>

  <hr>

  <h2>4. Staff Personnel Administration (<code>users</code>)</h2>
  <p>Staff members operate the internal business functions of THREAD. The Admin developer built the complete operational lifecycle for managing these internal operators under <code>/api/v1/admin/staff</code>:</p>

