        <td><code>/api/v1/admin/staff/:id</code></td>
        <td>ADMIN</td>
        <td>Updates staff name, role, or status with before/after audit diff.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="badge badge-delete">DELETE</span></td>
        <td><code>/api/v1/admin/staff/:id</code></td>
        <td>ADMIN</td>
        <td>Deactivates (soft-deletes) staff member with audit log.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="badge badge-get">GET</span></td>
        <td><code>/api/v1/admin/audit-logs</code></td>
        <td>ADMIN</td>
        <td>Browses system audit log entries with action/actor filters.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><span class="badge badge-get">GET</span></td>
        <td><code>/api/v1/admin/analytics/summary</code></td>
        <td>ADMIN</td>
        <td>Returns executive platform metrics across users, audit, and orders.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr>

  <h2>10. Viva Presentation & Professor Defense Cheat Sheet</h2>
  <p>Use these concise answers when the course instructor or external examiner questions your implementation during the project defense:</p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Q1: What exact parts did you develop for the project?</strong><br>
    <em>"I owned the <strong>Admin</strong> domain (Developer D). I implemented the shared authentication foundation with bcrypt password hashing and JWT token issuance, the declarative RBAC system with custom <code>@Roles()</code> and <code>@Actor()</code> decorators, complete staff account administration (<code>/admin/staff</code>), the centralized audit log system with before/after state capture (<code>audit_logs</code> table and <code>/admin/audit-logs</code>), and real-time platform analytics (<code>/admin/analytics/summary</code>)."</em></p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Q2: How do you prevent privilege escalation during public registration?</strong><br>
    <em>"In <code>AuthService.register()</code>, the user creation strictly forces <code>role: UserRole.CUSTOMER</code>. Public registration cannot set any staff or admin roles. Internal accounts can only be provisioned by an authenticated administrator through <code>POST /admin/staff</code>, which is protected by <code>JwtAuthGuard</code> and <code>RolesGuard</code> with <code>@Roles([UserRole.ADMIN])</code>."</em></p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Q3: How and where did you implement multi-table database transactions?</strong><br>
    <em>"In <code>UsersService</code>, staff account creation, updates, and soft-deletions execute inside an atomic transaction using TypeORM's <code>this.dataSource.transaction(async manager => ...)</code>. The user table mutation and the audit log insertion use the same <code>EntityManager</code>. If the audit log fails to write, the user mutation rolls back immediately, ensuring no unaudited state changes can persist."</em></p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Q4: Why did you use JSONB for the before and after states in the audit log?</strong><br>
    <em>"PostgreSQL's <code>JSONB</code> binary format allows us to capture flexible entity snapshots without creating dedicated audit shadow tables for every single entity. It indexes efficiently and enables exact before/after state diffing for regulatory compliance."</em></p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Q5: How do you handle deactivation versus hard deletion?</strong><br>
    <em>"We implement soft deactivation by setting <code>status = UserStatus.INACTIVE</code>. Hard-deleting staff would violate foreign key constraints on historical orders, stock movements, and audit records. Furthermore, <code>JwtStrategy</code> checks <code>status = ACTIVE</code> on every single authenticated request, so deactivating a staff member instantly invalidates their access."</em></p>
  </div>

